    51 => [
        'title' => 'Fire at Lake Toba',
        'excerpt' => 'By Dr. Wilmar Eliaser Simandjorang, Dipl.Ec., M.Si — Forest and land fires recurred in the Tele Forest area, Sosor Tolong Village, Harian District, Samosir Regency, on 26 January 2026 at 4:52 p.m. WIB. The timing is noteworthy: the fire emerged early in the year, outside the period usually known to be prone, namely May to October. This fact should be read as a signal of rising ecological vulnerability around Lake Toba.',
        'content' => 'A January fire cannot simply be understood as a weather anomaly. It reflects an ecosystem growing increasingly fragile due to long-term pressure on forest cover and catchment areas. When forests catch fire easily even before the peak of the dry season, what needs to be evaluated is environmental carrying capacity and the effectiveness of its management policies. A serious concern is the lack of post-fire mitigation measures throughout the long 2025 dry season. To date, there has been no visible planned ecosystem rehabilitation, no strengthening of fire prevention systems, and no systematic community engagement. Fire response still tends to be reactive — focused on extinguishing rather than prevention and recovery. From a public policy perspective, such an approach leaves fundamental problems unresolved. Environmental protection should be placed as an integral part of development governance. Without sustained mitigation, recurring fires will become an almost inevitable consequence.',
    ],
